// each property's running weight is kept, and the new value is mixed in by
// weight / (running weight + weight), so weights need not sum to 1 and a
// property that only some of the samples animate is normalised over those
// (a clip that does not touch a property neither pulls it to zero nor
// dilutes the others). Floats and vectors mix linearly, quaternions by exact
// spherical interpolation along the shortest arc, colours linearly in linear
// light, and a bool or discrete value takes whichever side has the larger
// share (a step has no in-between). A transform mixes its three parts.
//
// ADDITIVE. 'addOnto' puts the difference of a sample from a reference
// sample on top of a base at a weight: the usual additive layer (a lean, a
// flinch, a breathing cycle). Numbers add the difference; a quaternion is
// slerp(identity, clip * reference^-1, weight) * base; a transform does each
// part so (its scale adds the difference); a bool or discrete value is
// replaced by the layer's when the weight reaches a half and the layer
// differs from its reference.

